# General Coding Formatting Rules

## Always Use Braces with Control Statements

**NEVER** use control statements (if, for, while, etc.) without braces, even for single-line actions. This prevents errors and improves code readability.

## Control Statement Brace Placement

**NEVER** add line breaks before opening braces in control flow statements. Opening braces must be on the same line as the control statement.

**CRITICAL**: This rule applies ONLY to control flow statements (if, while, switch, for, foreach, try, catch, etc.). Methods and classes use a different rule - they MUST have line breaks before opening braces (see below).

This applies to all control flow statements:
- `if` statements
- `foreach` loops
- `try` blocks
- `for` loops
- `while` loops
- `switch` statements
- `catch` blocks
- `do-while` loops
- Any other control flow constructs

### ❌ Incorrect - Line break before opening brace

```vala
// BAD - Line break before opening brace
if (condition)
{
    doSomething();
}

foreach (var item in items)
{
    process(item);
}

try
{
    riskyOperation();
}
catch (Error e)
{
    handleError(e);
}
```

### ✅ Correct - Opening brace on same line

```vala
// GOOD - Opening brace on same line
if (condition) {
    doSomething();
}

foreach (var item in items) {
    process(item);
}

try {
    riskyOperation();
} catch (Error e) {
    handleError(e);
}
```

## Method and Class Brace Placement

**ALWAYS** place opening braces on a new line after method declarations and class declarations for consistency and readability.

This applies to:
- Method declarations
- Class declarations
- Interface declarations
- Namespace declarations

